      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1884 the Catonsville Short Line Railroad cut travel to Baltimore to under 30 minutes, sparking a commuter boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Catonsville,_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Catonsville developed as a streetcar suburb of Baltimore, growing along Frederick Road toward the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catonsville%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first European settlers appeared around 1720, naming the area Johnnycake after a common frontier flatbread.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Catonsville,_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Founded in 1810 as a summer retreat, Catonsville’s tree‑lined streets and Victorian mansions still echo its genteel past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
